# Car Thing / superbird-console environment config.
#
# ⚠ This file must be RENAMED to `.superbird.conf.example` before publishing -- it is
# named `superbird.conf.example` (no leading dot) only because this build environment's
# sandbox hard-blocks every filesystem operation on a `.env*` path, including
# creating a template with no real secrets in it. `scripts/setup.sh` writes
# the real, gitignored `.env` (see .gitignore) the same way any other tool
# would -- that restriction is specific to the environment this file was
# authored in, not to the shipped repo.
#
# Copy to .env (or let `scripts/setup.sh` do it) and fill in what applies.
# `.env` is gitignored -- nothing here is meant to be committed with real
# values. See INSTALL.md for how this file gets loaded, and
# docs/operations-runbook.md's Configuration section for what each service
# does with these.

# --- REQUIRED -----------------------------------------------------------
# Both are auto-detected by `scripts/setup.sh` -- you shouldn't need to look
# these up by hand. Set them here only if detection was ambiguous or you're
# overriding it.

# adb serial of the Car Thing device. Only needed once a SECOND adb device
# (e.g. a phone) is ever attached to the same adb server -- with exactly one
# device attached, this can stay unset.
CAR_THING_SERIAL=

# Path to the adb binary that can see the device. Plain 'adb' on PATH works
# on Linux. On WSL2 the device is on the WINDOWS usb bus, so this must point
# at the Windows adb.exe, e.g.:
CAR_THING_ADB=/mnt/c/Users/YOURNAME/AppData/Local/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb.exe

# --- OPTIONAL: local LLM fleet -------------------------------------------
# Unset = the fleet screen reports itself unconfigured; nothing is probed.
# The Claude-monitoring half of this project (sessions, permissions) works
# fully with all three of these left blank.

# LAN host or IP running your fleet box (llama.cpp router + optional
# senior/pcreate lanes). Example uses an RFC 5737 documentation address --
# put your real host here, never commit a real one.
MB_HOST=192.0.2.10

# ssh alias (NOT a bare hostname) for firing fleet actions, resolved from
# your own ~/.ssh/config -- user, port and key stay there, never here.
MB_SSH_HOST=fleet-box

# LAN host or IP running a second, always-on generalist model probed for the
# FLEET screen's health lamp.
CODER_HOST=192.0.2.20

# --- OPTIONAL: sibling projects -------------------------------------------
# Two extra blocks in GET /state, both reported to nobody by default: the
# device app does not render them today, so leaving these unset costs you
# nothing. They exist for whatever you point them at -- neither is part of
# this repo, and neither is guessed from this checkout's location.

# Directory holding six state dirs named pending, in-progress, done, review,
# escalated, failed. Counted (non-dotfile entries) into /state's queue block.
# Unset = every count reports null with "QUEUE_ROOT not set", never 0.
QUEUE_ROOT=

# Script run as `<script> status`; its stdout becomes /state's obligations
# line verbatim. Invoked with execFile (argv array, no shell) and cached 30s.
# Unset = the line reports null with "OBLIGATIONS_SCRIPT not set".
OBLIGATIONS_SCRIPT=

# --- OPTIONAL: CONTROL grid -----------------------------------------------
# Directory holding YOUR OWN fleet-control scripts (router-control.sh,
# start-router.sh) -- substituted into services/deviceinfo/buttons.json's
# `${CONTROL_SCRIPTS_DIR}` tokens. Unset = those CONTROL buttons fail
# per-press with ENOENT instead of doing anything.
CONTROL_SCRIPTS_DIR=/home/YOURNAME/fleet-scripts
